On 21st December 2020 (winter solstice), the Sun will be in the constellation of Sagittarius. Planets are always near the ecliptic. The current 12 zodiac constellations can be roughly divided into 12 equal parts of 30 degrees each.
The conjunction will locate elongation $30.3^{\circ}$ East to the Sun. Therefore, the conjunction will be in the constellation of Capricornus (Cap).
\boxed{\text{Capricornus (Cap)}}

The temperature at which the cloud ceiling forms is given by the adiabatic relation:

$$
T_1 = T_0 \left( \frac{p_1}{p_0} \right)^{1 - \frac{1}{\chi}} = 279 \, \mathrm{K}
$$

\boxed{T_1 = 279 \, \mathrm{K}}

The temperature difference when the air ascends from the cloud ceiling to the mountain ridge is caused by two processes: adiabatic cooling to temperature $T_x$ and heating by $\Delta T$ due to condensation.

\[
T_2 = T_x + \Delta T \tag{3}
\]

\[
T_x = T_1 \left(\frac{p_2}{p_1}\right)^{1 - \frac{1}{\chi}} = 265\,\mathrm{K} \tag{4}
\]

For each kg of air, the heat produced by condensation is $L_v \cdot 2.45\,\mathrm{g} = 6.125\,\mathrm{kJ}$.

\[
\Delta T = \frac{6.125\,\mathrm{kJ}}{c_p} = 6.1\,\mathrm{K} \tag{5}
\]

\[
T_2 = 271\,\mathrm{K} \tag{6}
\]

\boxed{T_2 = 271\,\mathrm{K}}

The temperature behind the mountain is found from the adiabatic relation:

$$
T_3 = T_2 \left(\frac{p_3}{p_2}\right)^{1 - \frac{1}{\kappa}} = 300\,\mathrm{K}
$$

The air at station $M_3$ is warmer and drier than at station $M_0$. The increase in temperature is due to the release of latent heat during condensation of water vapour.

\boxed{T_3 = 300\,\mathrm{K}}

The uncertainty principle states that the minimum time $\Delta t$ and the minimum energy $\Delta E$ satisfy $\Delta E \Delta t \geq \hbar$. During this time, the neutrons move to the right a distance $\Delta x = v_x \Delta t \geq v_x  rac{\hbar}{\Delta E}$. The minimum neutron energy allowed in the cavity is $E_1$, so $\Delta E \approx E_1$. Therefore, the minimum time and minimum length required are

$$
t_q \approx  rac{\hbar}{E_1} = 0.4 	imes 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{s} = 0.4 \, \mathrm{ms}
$$

$$
L_q \approx v_x \frac{\hbar}{E_1} = 4 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{m} = 4 \, \mathrm{mm}
$$

\boxed{t_q \approx 0.4 \, \mathrm{ms}} \\
\boxed{L_q \approx 4 \, \mathrm{mm}}

A spiral trajectory occurs when the condition for a real minimum distance of approach fails. The minimum distance $r_{\min}$ is real under the condition:

$$
1 \geq \frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2 b^4}
$$

This gives the critical impact parameter:

$$
b \geq b_0 = \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/4}
$$

For $b < b_0$, the ion will descend along a spiral and collide with the atom, leading to charge exchange. The cross-sectional area for this process is:

$$
A = \pi b_0^2 = \pi \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/2}
$$

\boxed{A = \pi \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/2}}

